    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Kartli, the ancient kingdom of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#georgia">Georgia</a>, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mtskheta">Mtskheta</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>, and called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iberia">Iberia</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#roman-empire">Romans</a>.</li><li>It guarded the passes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caucasus">Caucasus</a> and balanced for centuries between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a> as client of one or the other.</li><li>It adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#christianity">Christianity</a> in the 4th century and produced the earliest Georgian inscriptions and literature.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pompey">Pompey the Great</a> fought his way into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iberia">Iberia</a> in 65 BCE while chasing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a>, defeating King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#artoces">Artoces</a> in battles on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kura-river">Mtkvari</a> and Aragvi rivers.</p></aside>
    </section>

