    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#alexander-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great.jpg" alt="Alexander the Great" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_Mosaic_detail_of_Alexander_the_Great_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iberia">Iberia</a>, known to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#georgians">Georgians</a> as Kartli, was the principal state of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#georgia">Georgia</a> in Antiquity, centred on the valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kura-river">Mtkvari</a> (Kura) river where it meets the Aragvi. The name Iberia was used by Greek and Roman writers and has no connection with the Iberian peninsula. Medieval Georgian tradition, preserved in the chronicle collection <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kartlis-tskhovreba">Kartlis Tskhovreba</a></em>, credits Pharnavaz I with uniting the land around 300 BCE, after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> and the campaigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, and with dividing it into provinces under governors called eristavis. These accounts were written many centuries later and mix history with legend, but archaeology confirms that a centralised kingdom with a fortified royal seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mtskheta">Mtskheta</a> and the acropolis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#armaztsikhe">Armaztsikhe</a> was taking shape in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a>. Its rise drew on the agricultural wealth of the Mtkvari valley and on control of routes through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caucasus">Caucasus</a>, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#darial-pass">Darial Pass</a>, which classical writers called the Caucasian Gates. By the first century BCE Iberia was a recognised state, described by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#strabo">Strabo</a> as a well-ordered kingdom whose people lived in towns with tiled roofs, market places and public buildings, in contrast to the pastoral peoples of the surrounding mountains.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#phasis-town"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phasis-town.jpg" alt="Phasis (town)" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#phasis-town">Phasis (town)</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Colchis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Samuel Butler, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#strabo">Strabo</a> describes Iberian society as divided into four classes: the royal family, from which the king and his deputy were chosen; the priests, who also handled disputes with neighbouring peoples; the soldiers and farmers; and the common people, whom he calls royal slaves, who did the work of the land. Most of the population farmed the fertile valleys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kura-river">Mtkvari</a>, Aragvi and Alazani, growing grain and vines, while herding was important in the uplands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iberia">Iberia</a> lay on a trade route that ran from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#black-sea">Black Sea</a> up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#phasis-town">Phasis</a> (Rioni) river through Colchis and over the Likhi range, then down the Mtkvari towards Albania and the Caspian, and it traded with the Greek cities of the Black Sea, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#armenia">Armenia</a> and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a>. Rich burials at Armaziskhevi and Bagineti near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mtskheta">Mtskheta</a>, containing gold jewellery, engraved gems and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a> vessels, reveal a wealthy aristocracy open to both Roman and Iranian fashions. Pre-Christian religion honoured deities such as Armazi, whose great idol, according to the chronicles, stood on the heights above Mtskheta, and Iranian and Zoroastrian influences were strong among the elite. A Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aramaic">Aramaic</a> bilingual inscription from Armazi, the epitaph of a noblewoman named Serapit, shows that Aramaic served as a written language before the Georgian alphabet was created.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#pompey"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pompey.jpg" alt="Pompey" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#pompey">Pompey</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(Venice)_Pompey_the_Great,_Museo_Archeologico_Nazionale.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Didier Descouens, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iberia">Iberia</a>’s mountain setting shaped its warfare. Its armies, drawn from the aristocracy and the farming population, combined infantry with heavy and light cavalry in the Iranian manner, and fortresses guarded the river valleys and the passes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#greater-caucasus">Greater Caucasus</a>. Control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#darial-pass">Darial Pass</a> made Iberia valuable to both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a>, since steppe peoples such as the Alans and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#huns">Huns</a> could ride through it into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#armenia">Armenia</a>, Iran and the Roman east. In 65 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pompey">Pompey</a>, pursuing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a>, invaded Iberia and defeated King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#artoces">Artoces</a> in battles on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kura-river">Mtkvari</a> and Aragvi, forcing him to submit. In the first century CE King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pharasmanes-of-khwarazm">Pharasmanes</a> I pursued an aggressive policy: in 35 CE, with Roman encouragement, he seized Armenia for his brother Mithridates and brought Sarmatian allies through the passes to defeat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#parthian-empire">Parthians</a>, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rhadamistus">Rhadamistus</a> later ruled Armenia for a few turbulent years. A Greek inscription found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mtskheta">Mtskheta</a> records that in 75 CE the Emperor Vespasian strengthened the city’s walls for King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mithridates-i-of-iberia">Mithridates of Iberia</a>, a friend of Rome. In late Antiquity Iberia fought repeatedly against Sasanian armies and against raiders from the north, often retreating into the mountains of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#georgia">Georgia</a> and seeking Roman and later Byzantine help.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#mirian-iii-of-iberia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mirian-iii-of-iberia.jpg" alt="Mirian III of Iberia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#mirian-iii-of-iberia">Mirian III of Iberia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Mirian,_a_17th-century_fresco_from_Svetitskhoveli_Cathedral_(Mtskheta,_Georgia).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kober, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iberia">Iberia</a> was a hereditary monarchy, ruled in turn by dynasties that Georgian tradition calls the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pharnavazid-dynasty">Pharnavazids</a>, Arsacids and, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mirian-iii-of-iberia">Mirian III</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chosroid-dynasty">Chosroids</a>, although the lists of early kings are partly legendary. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#strabo">Strabo</a> notes that the second in rank after the king served as judge and commander of the army. Inscriptions and burials confirm a high office of <em>pitiakhsh</em>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> or deputy, whose family was buried at Armaziskhevi, and the title <em>eristavi</em>, ‘head of the people’, was used for regional governors. Great noble houses held hereditary lands and armed followings, and the balance between king and aristocracy shaped Iberian politics throughout its history. Externally, the kings had to manoeuvre between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pompey">Pompey</a>’s campaign Iberia became a client of Rome, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pharasmanes-ii-of-iberia">Pharasmanes II</a> visited Rome, probably under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#antoninus-pius">Antoninus Pius</a>, where he was received with honour and allowed to sacrifice on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#capitoline-hill">Capitol</a>. In the third century the Sasanian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shapur-i">Shapur I</a> listed Iberia among his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dominion">dominions</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#peace-of-nisibis-299">Peace of Nisibis</a> of 298 or 299 gave Rome the right to invest Iberian kings. After 363, when Rome gave up its claims in the region, Iberia passed under Sasanian supremacy, and Persian officials increasingly supervised the royal court.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#svetitskhoveli-cathedral"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/svetitskhoveli-cathedral.jpg" alt="Svetitskhoveli Cathedral" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#svetitskhoveli-cathedral">Svetitskhoveli Cathedral</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Svetitskhoveli_Cathedral_09.23_(3).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">This Photo was taken by Supanut Arunoprayote. Feel free to use any of my images, but please mention me as the author ..., CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iberia">Iberia</a> laid the foundations of Georgian statehood, religion and letters. Its conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#christianity">Christianity</a> under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mirian-iii-of-iberia">Mirian III</a>, traditionally through the preaching of St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#saint-nino">Nino</a>, a woman from Cappadocia, is usually dated to the 320s or 330s, which makes Iberia one of the first states, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#armenia">Armenia</a>, to adopt Christianity as its official religion; the church historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rufinus-of-aquileia">Rufinus of Aquileia</a>, writing c. 402, recorded the story of a captive woman who converted the king and queen. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#svetitskhoveli-cathedral">Svetitskhoveli Cathedral</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mtskheta">Mtskheta</a> stands on the site of the first royal church and remains one of the principal cathedrals of the Georgian Church. The Georgian alphabet, in its earliest form called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asomtavruli">Asomtavruli</a>, was in use by the fifth century, as shown by inscriptions at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bir-el-qutt">Bir el Qutt</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bethlehem">Bethlehem</a> and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bolnisi-sioni">Bolnisi Sioni</a> church, dated 493–494, and the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#martyrdom-of-the-holy-queen-shushanik">Martyrdom of the Holy Queen Shushanik</a></em>, written in the late fifth century, is the oldest surviving work of Georgian literature. Iberian monks and pilgrims linked the kingdom with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#syria">Syria</a> and the wider Christian world. Later Georgian states, above all the Bagrationi monarchy that united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#georgia">Georgia</a> in the eleventh century, saw themselves as heirs of Kartli, and Mtskheta, where Georgian kings were crowned and buried, remained the spiritual centre of the nation.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#shapur-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shapur-ii.jpg" alt="Shapur II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#shapur-ii">Shapur II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Shapur_II_the_Great_in_the_Metropolitan_Museum_of_Art.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iberia">Iberia</a>’s independence eroded steadily under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> withdrew from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caucasus">Caucasus</a> in 363, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shapur-ii">Shapur II</a> installed his own candidate on the throne, and for a time the kingdom was divided between Roman and Persian protégés. In the later fifth century King Vakhtang I Gorgasali, who according to tradition reigned from c. 447 to c. 502 or 522, tried to restore Iberian power, strengthened the Church under its own catholicos and is traditionally credited with founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>, which his successor Dachi made the capital. In 482 he joined an Armenian revolt against the Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#peroz-i">Peroz</a>, but the rebels were defeated, and Georgian tradition records that he later died fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persians">Persians</a>. In the 520s King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gurgenes">Gurgenes</a> rose against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kavad-i">Kavad I</a> and appealed to the Byzantine Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#justin-i">Justin I</a>, but when Persian armies invaded he fled with his family and nobles to Lazica, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#procopius">Procopius</a>. Persian control tightened during the long wars between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a>. When King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bakur-iii-of-iberia">Bakur III</a> died around 580, the Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hormizd-iv">Hormizd IV</a> abolished the monarchy and governed Iberia through a <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marzban">marzban</a></em>, while the great nobles kept their estates. In 588 the nobility, with Byzantine support, chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guaram-i-of-iberia">Guaram I</a> as presiding prince, beginning the Principality of Iberia, and Georgian kingship was restored only in the ninth century.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iberia">Iberia</a>, or Kartli, was the cradle of Georgian statehood: it guarded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caucasus">Caucasus</a> passes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a> for nearly nine centuries, became one of the first Christian kingdoms in the 4th century and produced the earliest Georgian script and literature before Persia abolished its monarchy.</em></p>

